{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfgRPCah8gQPBL25AS8zX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote 8 test statistics to `iter_2/critique/test_stats/`. I ran each one on the observed data and on a copy with random responses. All return a finite number, and the slowest takes about 0.12 s per call, well under the 5 s limit. I haven't seen p-values yet; the pipeline produces them when it runs the check.\n\nThe model assumes heads and tails are interchangeable. It looks at a sequence's running heads-minus-tails spread, how often it switches, how many different three-flip chunks it has, and whether it was built by copying or mirroring. It doesn't look at where in a sequence a pattern sits. Each statistic tests one thing outside that:\n\n| Statistic | What it tests | Observed |\n|---|---|---|\n| `more_heads_chosen_rate` | Do people prefer sequences with more heads? The model can't express that. | 0.500 |\n| `shorter_longest_run_equal_switches` | When two sequences switch equally often, do people avoid the one with the longer streak? | 0.617 |\n| `balanced_count_equal_span` | When the running-tally spread is equal, do people use the final heads/tails count? | 0.363 |\n| `final_run_shorter_chosen` | Do people dislike a streak at the end more than one elsewhere? | 0.698 |\n| `item_overdispersion_len8` | Are people more split, or more in agreement, on individual 8-flip pairs than the model predicts? | 0.063 |\n| `agreement_sd_structurally_neutral` | How much people differ in agreeing with the majority, on pairs where the two sequences don't differ in chunk variety or copy/mirror structure. | 0.224 |\n| `switch_balance_trait_correlation` | Do people who prefer more switching also prefer balanced counts? The model treats these person-level preferences as unrelated. | 0.818 |\n| `short_vs_long_item_strength` | Are short pairs (4 flips or fewer) judged more or less decisively than long pairs (7 or more), compared with what the model predicts? | −0.033 |\n\nThe last-round critique said people differ more in how often they agree with the majority than the old model allowed. The incumbent explains that with each person's sensitivity to pattern structure. `agreement_sd_structurally_neutral` checks that explanation: it looks only at pairs where pattern sensitivity has nothing to act on. If people still differ more than the model predicts there, the extra variation isn't about pattern structure.\n\nOn the random-response copy, `switch_balance_trait_correlation` still came out at 0.55. Many pairs differ in both switches and balance, so the same choices feed both rates. The model's simulated data share that overlap, so the comparison is still fair. Just read the statistic against the model's average, not against zero."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6177,"cache_read_input_tokens":45824,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6177,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"63d82dcd-088c-45d1-a451-a51906250051","uuid":"28049cd1-4e3c-48d7-9040-972049960ff8","timestamp":"2026-10-04T02:38:30.904Z","request_id":"req_011CfgRPCDcdY3qvK2R4h2F1"}
